\begin{comment}\begin{section}{Conclusion}



We have explicitly computed the spectrum data for the Jucys-Murphys elements of $\tilde{S}_n$ on genuine projective representations; utilised very different methods from previous attempts. Our results depend on Dirac cohomology and Garcia and Procesi's branching rules to show the branching rules for $\mathcal{T}_n$. Then, again, using Dirac cohomology to describe two different ways to build up the Casimir element $\Omega_{\tilde{S}_n}$ of $\tilde{S}_n$. This leads to a description of the action of the Jucys-Murphy elements.  Then one can use this description to construct explicit models for the genuine projective supermodules of $\tilde{S}_n$. Looking forward the author believes that this method will also provide a very similar result for type B. Jucys-Murphy elements for type B have been discussed by Ram \cite{R97} although this method would require slight reformations of Ram's Jucys-Murphy elements. 

\end{section}\end{comment}





